    {
      "slug": "reddit-voluntary-overtime-labor-board",
      "script": "Voluntary means unpaid? My manager scheduled unpaid weekend inventory counts as \"voluntary team spirit.\" Skip one, lose the good shifts for a month. So I saved every text ordering us in on days off and every pay stub showing zero overtime. After four months, I had four weekends documented — timestamps, texts, badge scans. Very spirited paperwork. I mailed the whole packet to our state labor board as a wage complaint and named the entire inventory team. Two weeks later, an investigator called the store. Our district manager arrived unannounced with printed labor code sections. Then my manager had to explain to his boss why six employees had four months of unpaid overtime. Within the month, the company cut backdated checks with interest for every person on that weekend list. How voluntary did those checks look?",
      "editor_note": "Punched up to a three-word question hook, compressed the evidence escalation, added one dry paperwork aside, and landed on the backdated-check reversal while preserving every fact, number, shot phrase, and query."
    },
    {
      "slug": "reddit-blender-serial-number-security",
      "script": "She grabbed the wrong employee? I was off in sweatpants, holding protein bars, when a woman grabbed my arm in the electronics aisle. She demanded to know why the store wouldn't take her opened blender back without a box. I told her three times I did not work there. She decided I was the slowest employee she had ever met. So I walked her straight to the actual return counter and told the real employee she needed a manager override because she was very insistent. Then I stepped back. The employee checked the blender's serial number. It matched one reported stolen from the store two towns over the week before. Security was already walking over when her gift receipt story started. I put down my protein bars and picked a different checkout line. Who did that blender actually belong to?",
      "editor_note": "Punched up to a five-word question hook, tightened the mistaken-employee escalation, kept the crime story completely straight, and landed on the stolen blender while preserving every fact, number, shot phrase, and query."
    }
